<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stendhal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1783">1783</a>–<a href="/years/1842">1842</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Marie-Henri Beyle</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stendhal was the pen name of Marie-Henri Beyle, a French writer born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grenoble">Grenoble</a> in 1783, best known for the novels The Red and the Black (1830) and The Charterhouse of Parma (1839) and considered one of the early practitioners of realism. He served in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>'s administration and was part of the army in the 1812 invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where he witnessed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fire-of-moscow-1812">burning of Moscow</a> and the winter retreat. After the fall of Napoleon he settled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a> until 1821, and from 1830 he was French consul at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trieste">Trieste</a> and Civitavecchia. His book De l'amour introduced the concept of crystallisation in passionate love. He died in 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stendhal.jpg" alt="Stendhal" width="330" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Olof Johan Södermark · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Stendhal.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
